The useful version of your “death by 1,000 cuts” idea is 1,000 legitimate micro-signals, not trying to hide automation from TikTok. TikTok explicitly prohibits automation, spam, fake engagement, and manipulating engagement signals, so I would not build the growth system around staying below detection thresholds. 

What I would build is a slow-burn swarm of real contact points: posts, replies, stitches, creator mentions, QR jokes, recurring lore, search terms, profile visits, ambassadors, comment callbacks, and occasional high-concept swings. TikTok’s current business guidance is very aligned with that: consistent TikTok-native content, creator/UGC participation, community interaction, experimentation, and then amplifying what already proves itself organically. 

For this account specifically, I’d do this:

* Stop treating follower count as the main objective. You already have ~3.4K followers, but essentially no visible content history/likes yet. The immediate job is making the account look like something people are discovering early, not something trying to acquire followers.
* Do not keep aggressively following people. The ~3,047 following / ~3,440 follower ratio can read like follow-for-follow. Just stop pushing that lever and let content gradually change the profile’s shape.
* Turn “The Experiments” into the spine of the account. TikTok is actively pushing episodic/micro-series storytelling as a discovery format, so our numbered Wild Hawg experiments are unusually well suited to the platform right now. 
* Name the community. A group of wild boars is a sounder. That is almost too perfect. Followers become The Sounder. Early active people become Founding Sounder. You can say things like “The Sounder found Experiment 0055 faster than expected.”
* Build recurring vocabulary: The Sounder, REWILD, Hawg Index, Experiment ####, Control Group, Human Verified, Subject, Field Report. Repetition creates recognition without having to yell the product name every post.
* Use a 80/15/5 content mix. About 80% should be repeatable low-cost experiments, absurd observations, photo posts, comment replies, and Wild Hawg lore. About 15% should be creator/community participation: Stitch, Duet, reply-to-comment, ambassadors, people testing things. About 5% should be “nukes”: unusually good concepts, bigger shoots, weird public experiments, high-production reveals.
* Post consistently rather than dumping. I’d start around 1–2 strong posts per day for the first couple of weeks. TikTok’s current guidance emphasizes consistency but explicitly says brands do not have to post daily just for the sake of volume. 
* Make comments part of the content architecture. Every post gets one deliberate pinned comment, but not always a CTA. Sometimes it should create missing information: “0056 produced an unexpected result.” Sometimes it should invite participation: “Report Hawg Index below.” Sometimes it should punish curiosity jokingly: “Opening the comments invalidated your control status.”
* Mine every good comment for another post. TikTok explicitly recommends Reply, Stitch, and Duet as native community mechanisms.  One funny audience comment should be capable of generating Experiment 0056, 0057, and 0058.
* Comment outward as Wild Hawg, but behave like a person. Find fitness, outdoors, gaming, military-humor, energy-product, hunting/fishing, startup, gym, and meme accounts where Wild Hawg actually has something funny or useful to say. No canned “🔥🔥 check us out” garbage. A genuinely funny Wild Hawg comment that gets 2,000 likes underneath someone else’s viral post is a free billboard.
* Recruit micro-creators manually. Not 500 spam DMs. Find 5–10 genuinely fitting people per week and send individually relevant messages. Give them a premise, not a corporate script: “We’re launching an energy gummy and conducting stupid internet experiments. Want to be Subject 0068?”
* Reward participation with status, not just discounts. “Founding Sounder,” numbered subject IDs, Hawg Index results, reposts, hidden QR rewards, early flavor votes, weird certificates, future product drops. Identity is cheaper and often stickier than coupons.
* Make profile visitors immediately understand the rabbit hole. Pin three posts: START HERE / Experiment 0047, the QR dreamcatcher experiment, and one post that reveals “Wild Hawg = energy gummies.” Mystery attracts the visit; clarity converts the follow.
* Change the bio from generic launch language into the universe. Something like: Energy gummies. 🐗 // Currently conducting internet experiments // JOIN THE SOUNDER // Coming soon. Then put collabs/ambassadors in a link or a lower-priority line rather than making the whole profile feel like recruitment.
* Give TikTok clear semantic signals. Captions should naturally contain phrases like Wild Hawg energy gummies, caffeine gummy, internet experiment, bot test, wild boar, etc. The joke remains strange for humans while the content remains classifiable for machines.
* Keep the actual brand underneath the weirdness. Eventually every strange thread needs to lead back toward one simple proposition: portable energy, no can, REWILD. Otherwise you can accidentally build a successful meme account that nobody associates with buying Wild Hawg.
* Use occasional product breadcrumbs before a full reveal. “VARIABLE ADDED: CAFFEINE.” “Subject consumed prototype 7B.” “Control group requested another gummy.” Let the audience discover what the company sells instead of repeatedly announcing it.
* Create participation artifacts. QR codes, hidden text, screenshot challenges, “comment without telling the others,” choose-the-next-experiment polls, fake lab reports, Hawg Index scores. These give people something to do, not merely something to watch.
* Make sharing socially useful. “Send this to the most bot-like person you know.” “Your friend needs human verification.” “Tag someone whose Hawg Index is concerning.” The share itself should complete the joke.
* Seed unanswered questions intentionally. There is never an Experiment 0049. Someone wears “SUBJECT 0082” before 0082 exists. A post references the “Kansas Incident” and nobody explains it. Do not fabricate consequential facts; fabricate harmless lore.
* Let some audience theories become canon. If a commenter invents something funnier than we did, Wild Hawg replies: “Correct.” Now people learn that participating can literally change the universe.
* Occasionally break the format. If every post says EXPERIMENT, the pattern becomes wallpaper. Every 7–10 posts, interrupt it with something like a dead-serious boar video captioned only: He knows.
* Use real faces eventually. TikTok still rewards authenticity/community presence, and Wild Hawg will ultimately benefit from founders, ambassadors, gym people, veterans, outdoors people, etc. appearing inside the mythology rather than keeping everything as designed graphics. 
* Only put money behind winners. If you eventually use Promote/Spark Ads, boost posts that already showed unusual organic behavior instead of paying to rescue weak creative. TikTok itself recommends using organic performance to identify what deserves amplification. 

And I’d run the whole thing through our Delta logic. Every 48 hours, track views → profile visits → follows → comments → shares → saves → repeat commenters. Don’t ask merely “Did this post perform?” Ask where did the chain break?

High views + low profile visits = entertaining but insufficient curiosity.
High profile visits + low follows = profile/identity problem.
High comments + low shares = participatory but not transmissible.
High shares + low follows = meme stronger than brand.
Repeat commenters appearing = The Sounder is forming.

That last metric is the one I’d particularly watch. A cult-like brand following begins when you stop seeing only more people and start seeing the same people returning, recognizing one another, speaking the language, and carrying the joke for you.

So the operating doctrine becomes:

Slow burn: hundreds of authentic small touches.
Mongol retreat: audience action advances the story.
Occasional nuke: a concept good enough to escape the existing audience.
Stable center: Wild Hawg / energy / REWILD.
Emergent perimeter: let the internet help invent everything around it.

That is a much stronger version of “under the radar”: not invisible to TikTok—native enough that the platform has legitimate reasons to keep carrying you.